.TH "DEVCLI\-UPGRADE" "1" "" "devcli" "Dedalus Manual"
.SH "NAME"
devcli\-upgrade \- upgrade devcli to the latest release
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B devcli upgrade
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Replaces this devcli executable with the latest published release, after verifying the download against the checksums published alongside it.
.PP
A devcli installed by a package manager is never replaced: that manager owns the installed files, and overwriting them leaves its database describing a file that is no longer there, so the command prints how to upgrade it instead.
.PP
devcli also checks for a newer release at most once a day and mentions one on standard error. That check is skipped when either standard output or standard error is not a terminal, when CI is set, when running under sudo, and when NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER or DEVCLI_NO_UPDATE_NOTIFIER is set in the environment.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Report the installed and latest versions and exit, without installing anything.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
The command completed successfully.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The command failed. The reason is reported on standard error.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The command was invoked incorrectly (missing or invalid arguments).
.PP
When devcli cannot perform the upgrade itself (a project dependency, a cache such as npx, a package manager it cannot find) it prints what to run instead and exits 0, having changed nothing. Run devcli upgrade \-\-check first to learn whether a newer release exists.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR devcli (1),
.BR devcli\-uninstall (1)
